      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "                                                        QUOROM READS/WRITES\nThe Setup\n\nYou have N replicas. When reading or writing, you don't need ALL replicas to respond — just a majority (or some configurable number).\n\nW = minimum number of replicas that must acknowledge a write before it's considered successful\nR = minimum number of replicas you must read from before returning a result\nN = total number of replicas\n\nThe Magic Formula: W + R > N\n\nIf W + R > N, then any quorum read is guaranteed to overlap with any quorum write. At least one node that received the latest write is in every \nread quorum.\n\nPROOF:\n\nLast write confirmed by W nodes (they have the latest version)\nRead queries R nodes\nW + R > N means W + R ≥ N + 1\nAt most N - W nodes didn't receive the latest write\nYour read queries R nodes. Since R > N - W, your read must include at least one node that received the write.\nThat node has the latest value → you get the latest value.\nCommon Configurations for N=3\n\nWITH 3 REPLICAS, MOST SYSTEMS DEFAULT TO THIS:\n\nConfiguration 1: W=1, R=1\n\nW + R = 2 ≤ 3 = N → NOT a quorum. No consistency guarantee.\nWrite to 1 node. Read from 1 node. They might not overlap.\nPros: Fastest possible — one node for reads, one for writes\nCons: No consistency. You might read stale data.\nUse for: Maximum throughput when stale reads are acceptable (analytics, metrics)\n\n\n\nConfiguration 2: W=2, R=2 (most common)\n\nW + R = 4 > 3 = N → Quorum achieved. Strong consistency.\nWrite to 2 of 3. Read from 2 of 3. Always overlap on at least 1 node.\nPros: Strong consistency. Can tolerate 1 node failure.\nCons: Writes and reads both need 2 nodes to respond.\nUse for: Most use cases — balanced consistency and availability.\n\n\n\nConfiguration 3: W=3, R=1\n\nW + R = 4 > 3 = N → Quorum achieved.\nWrite to ALL 3 nodes. Read from just 1.\nPros: Super fast reads (only 1 node needed). Strong consistency.\nCons: Slow writes (all 3 must respond). If any node is down, writes fail.\nUse for: Read-heavy workloads where writes are rare and reads must be fast.\n\n\n\nConfiguration 4: W=1, R=3\n\nW + R = 4 > 3 = N → Quorum achieved.\nWrite to 1 node (fast write). Read from all 3.\nPros: Fast writes. Strong consistency on reads.\nCons: Slow reads (must query all 3). If any node is down, reads fail.\nUse for: Write-heavy workloads with occasional strong-consistency reads.\n\n                                                                    \n                                                                      TUNING IN CASSANDRA\n\nCassandra lets you specify consistency per query:\n\nsql\n-- Write with quorum (2 of 3 nodes must confirm)\nINSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES ('123', 'm@example.com') USING CONSISTENCY QUORUM;\n\n-- Read with quorum (2 of 3 nodes must respond)\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '123' USING CONSISTENCY QUORUM;\n\n-- Write with just 1 node (fastest, weakest)\nINSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES ('123', 'm@example.com') USING CONSISTENCY ONE;\n\n-- Read requiring ALL nodes\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '123' USING CONSISTENCY ALL;\n\n\nThis per-operation tunability is why Cassandra is so flexible. A payment service can use QUORUM for financial data. A recommendation service \ncan use ONE for slightly stale recommendations. Same cluster, different guarantees.\n\nThe Sloppy Quorum and Hinted Handoff\n\nWhat if some nodes in the natural quorum (the nodes that normally own this data) are down? You need W writes but only W-1 nodes are available.\n\nSloppy quorum: Accept the write on a \"healthy but non-owner\" node as a temporary measure. When the original owner comes back online, the tem-\nporary node forwards the write (this is called hinted handoff — the temporary node \"hints\" that it has data belonging to another node).\n\nNormal owners of key K: Node A, Node B, Node C\nNode C is down. Write requires W=2.\n\nSloppy quorum: write to Node A, Node B (owners), and Node D (non-owner)\nNode A and Node B confirm → W=2 satisfied, write succeeds\nNode D stores the write with a \"hint\": \"This belongs to Node C, deliver when C recovers\"\n\nNode C recovers → Node D sends the hint → Node C applies the missed write\n\nThis is how Cassandra and DynamoDB maintain availability even when nodes are down. It's an AP trade-off — you accept a write on a non-owner \nto maintain availability, and correct the placement later.\n\nQuorum and Latency\n\nQuorum reads/writes have a natural latency optimization: you don't wait for the SLOWEST node, you wait for the W-th or R-th fastest.\n\nWith W=2, N=3: you send writes to all 3 nodes in parallel. As soon as 2 confirm, the write is done. The 3rd node might be slow — you don't care. \nThis is called HEDGED REQUESTS — sending to more nodes than you need and taking the first W responses.\n\nSend write to Node A, B, C simultaneously\nNode B responds in 5ms ✅ (1 of 2 needed)\nNode A responds in 8ms ✅ (2 of 2 needed) → DONE. Return to client.\nNode C responds in 150ms → ignored, already done\n\nP99 latency is dramatically better because you're not waiting for the slow outlier.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                        QUOROM READS/WRITES\nThe Setup\n\nYou have N replicas. When reading or writing, you don't need ALL replicas to respond — just a majority (or some configurable number).\n\nW = minimum number of replicas that must acknowledge a write before it's considered successful\nR = minimum number of replicas you must read from before returning a result\nN = total number of replicas\n\nThe Magic Formula: W + R > N\n\nIf W + R > N, then any quorum read is guaranteed to overlap with any quorum write. At least one node that received the latest write is in every \nread quorum.\n\nPROOF:\n\nLast write confirmed by W nodes (they have the latest version)\nRead queries R nodes\nW + R > N means W + R ≥ N + 1\nAt most N - W nodes didn't receive the latest write\nYour read queries R nodes. Since R > N - W, your read must include at least one node that received the write.\nThat node has the latest value → you get the latest value.\nCommon Configurations for N=3\n\nWITH 3 REPLICAS, MOST SYSTEMS DEFAULT TO THIS:\n\nConfiguration 1: W=1, R=1\n\nW + R = 2 ≤ 3 = N → NOT a quorum. No consistency guarantee.\nWrite to 1 node. Read from 1 node. They might not overlap.\nPros: Fastest possible — one node for reads, one for writes\nCons: No consistency. You might read stale data.\nUse for: Maximum throughput when stale reads are acceptable (analytics, metrics)\n\n\n\nConfiguration 2: W=2, R=2 (most common)\n\nW + R = 4 > 3 = N → Quorum achieved. Strong consistency.\nWrite to 2 of 3. Read from 2 of 3. Always overlap on at least 1 node.\nPros: Strong consistency. Can tolerate 1 node failure.\nCons: Writes and reads both need 2 nodes to respond.\nUse for: Most use cases — balanced consistency and availability.\n\n\n\nConfiguration 3: W=3, R=1\n\nW + R = 4 > 3 = N → Quorum achieved.\nWrite to ALL 3 nodes. Read from just 1.\nPros: Super fast reads (only 1 node needed). Strong consistency.\nCons: Slow writes (all 3 must respond). If any node is down, writes fail.\nUse for: Read-heavy workloads where writes are rare and reads must be fast.\n\n\n\nConfiguration 4: W=1, R=3\n\nW + R = 4 > 3 = N → Quorum achieved.\nWrite to 1 node (fast write). Read from all 3.\nPros: Fast writes. Strong consistency on reads.\nCons: Slow reads (must query all 3). If any node is down, reads fail.\nUse for: Write-heavy workloads with occasional strong-consistency reads.\n\n                                                                    \n                                                                      TUNING IN CASSANDRA\n\nCassandra lets you specify consistency per query:\n\nsql\n-- Write with quorum (2 of 3 nodes must confirm)\nINSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES ('123', 'm@example.com') USING CONSISTENCY QUORUM;\n\n-- Read with quorum (2 of 3 nodes must respond)\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '123' USING CONSISTENCY QUORUM;\n\n-- Write with just 1 node (fastest, weakest)\nINSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES ('123', 'm@example.com') USING CONSISTENCY ONE;\n\n-- Read requiring ALL nodes\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '123' USING CONSISTENCY ALL;\n\n\nThis per-operation tunability is why Cassandra is so flexible. A payment service can use QUORUM for financial data. A recommendation service \ncan use ONE for slightly stale recommendations. Same cluster, different guarantees.\n\nThe Sloppy Quorum and Hinted Handoff\n\nWhat if some nodes in the natural quorum (the nodes that normally own this data) are down? You need W writes but only W-1 nodes are available.\n\nSloppy quorum: Accept the write on a \"healthy but non-owner\" node as a temporary measure. When the original owner comes back online, the tem-\nporary node forwards the write (this is called hinted handoff — the temporary node \"hints\" that it has data belonging to another node).\n\nNormal owners of key K: Node A, Node B, Node C\nNode C is down. Write requires W=2.\n\nSloppy quorum: write to Node A, Node B (owners), and Node D (non-owner)\nNode A and Node B confirm → W=2 satisfied, write succeeds\nNode D stores the write with a \"hint\": \"This belongs to Node C, deliver when C recovers\"\n\nNode C recovers → Node D sends the hint → Node C applies the missed write\n\nThis is how Cassandra and DynamoDB maintain availability even when nodes are down. It's an AP trade-off — you accept a write on a non-owner \nto maintain availability, and correct the placement later.\n\nQuorum and Latency\n\nQuorum reads/writes have a natural latency optimization: you don't wait for the SLOWEST node, you wait for the W-th or R-th fastest.\n\nWith W=2, N=3: you send writes to all 3 nodes in parallel. As soon as 2 confirm, the write is done. The 3rd node might be slow — you don't care. \nThis is called HEDGED REQUESTS — sending to more nodes than you need and taking the first W responses.\n\nSend write to Node A, B, C simultaneously\nNode B responds in 5ms ✅ (1 of 2 needed)\nNode A responds in 8ms ✅ (2 of 2 needed) → DONE. Return to client.\nNode C responds in 150ms → ignored, already done\n\nP99 latency is dramatically better because you're not waiting for the slow outlier.",
